  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front brings showers and a few thunderstorms\ntoday into Friday morning.\n\nA mid to upper jet streak will bring forcing along a surface\ncold front as it drops southeastward over the next 36 to 48\nhours. This combination of a jet and surface front will initiate\nadditional showers and a few thunderstorms along and on the\nnorth side of the front primarily mid afternoon into the\novernight tonight. The line of heavy showers and storms that\nmoved from southwest PA into western MD late last evening and\novernight has since weakened considerably and is now a broken \nline of showers in western MD and northeastern WV. \n\nA strong mid to upper jet will swing by to the north today into\ntonight and put us in the right entrance. This would indicate\ngood upward motion with developing convection later today into\ntonight. The greatest CAPE will likely be around 1000-1500 J/kg\nacross central VA where the most heating and highest low- level\nmoisture content is anticipated.\n \nSome storms may tend to cluster heading into this evening given\nforcing along the front and increased flow aloft, but the \nmodest CAPE/DCAPE should keep any severe weather threat rather \nlimited for wind and hail. Still, can't rule out a briefly \nstronger, more organized rotating storm in the vicinity of the \nfront/wave in the evening across central VA to southern MD.\n\nThe front will slowly but steadily drop southward heading into\nFriday. Some shower activity may linger or redevelop in the\nvicinity of the front near the I-64 corridor, but most of the\ntime/area should be dry Friday unless the front slows down much\nmore significantly.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers and thunderstorms possible on Sunday,\nfollowed by drier conditions early next week.\n\nA weak cold front passes through the forecast area on Sunday, \nresulting in a few possible thunderstorms in the afternoon into the \nevening. While chances for these storms to be severe remain low, a \nfew strong storms with gusty winds cannot be ruled out. Come Monday, \nupper level ridging starts building over the east coast accompanied \nby surface high pressure overhead. Primarily dry conditions are \nexpected on Monday, although an isolated rain shower cannot be ruled \nout along the western portions of the forecast area as a cold front \ntracks across the Great Lakes. Precipitation chances increase by \nmidweek as another cold front tracks across the forecast area.\n\nHigh temperatures will be in the 80s for most (70s mountains) each \nday with high humidity values yielding heat indices in the low 90s \nfor those east of the Blue Ridge. Wednesday will be the warmest day \nwith highs in the upper 80s to low 90s and heat indices in the upper \n90s.",
